      "snapshot": "Short Range Forecast Discussion\nNWS Weather Prediction Center College Park MD\n258 AM EST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nValid 12Z Sun Nov 01 2026 - 12Z Tue Nov 03 2026\n\n...Impactful flash flooding expected in the Great Basin and Four\nCorners today...\n\n...Heavy rainfall threat along the east coast of Florida through\nMonday...\n\n...Intensifying low pressure system brings gusty winds, locally\nheavy rain, and severe weather to the Great Plains...\n\nThe forecast remains on track for a low pressure system developing\noff Baja California to merge with a broad upper-level trough\nentering the West Coast. This merger will usher in a round of\nwidespread unsettled weather through much of the interior West\ntoday, shifting east into the Great Plains on Monday. For today,\nthe main impacts with this system remain focused over the Southwest\nand Four Corners as rainfall coverage and intensity steadily\nincrease this morning. Storm total rainfall upwards of 2-4\" along\nupslope-facing terrain in these regions could lead to numerous\ninstances of flash flooding today, especially for areas of\nsaturated soils brought by recent heavy rainfall.\n\nBy tomorrow, a rather strong and deep cyclone is forecast to spin\nup rapidly over the Central High Plains. Much of the Northern\nPlains will wake up to increasing winds, showers, and embedded\nthunderstorms to kick off the work week. The Central and Southern\nPlains can expect to see thunderstorms potentially turn severe\nlater on Monday with locally heavy rainfall possible. On Tuesday,\na sweeping cold front will shift the focus for unsettled weather\nfrom the Plains into the Mid/Lower Mississippi Valley. Portions of\nthe Central Rockies will also have to contend with heavy snow on\nthe back side of this system, where a Winter Storm Warning and a\nhandful of Winter Weather Advisories are in effect.\n\nMeanwhile, showers and embedded thunderstorms over the Southeast will\ncontinue to shift toward the Florida Panhandle today. Persistent\nonshore flow from a large high pressure system sliding off the East\nCoast will bring heavy rainfall threat along the east coast of the\nFlorida Peninsula Sunday and Monday with isolated to scattered flash\nflooding potential. In contrast, the same high pressure system\nwill provide dry weather for much of the eastern U.S. with colder\nthan normal conditions in New England but milder than normal to the\nsouth.\n\nAsherman/Kong\n\n\nGraphics available at\n<a href=\"https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/basicwx/basicwx_ndfd.php\"><u>https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/basicwx/basicwx_ndfd.php</u></a>"
    },
